If you're learning React Native with Expo, sooner or later you'll want an **APK** to install your app on a phone or share it with someone. The official route is **EAS Build**, Expo's cloud service, but the free plan has a queue and a monthly build limit.

The good news: you can build the APK on your own computer, for free. In this note I'll show you three ways, from the simplest to the most complete.

## What you need

- An Expo project (SDK 50 or later works the same).
- **Android Studio**, which includes the Android SDK: [download it here](https://developer.android.com/studio/install).
- **Java 17** (the JDK bundled with Android Studio works).
- The `ANDROID_HOME` variable pointing to the SDK. On macOS it's usually `~/Library/Android/sdk`.

## Step 1: generate the native code with expo prebuild

Expo creates the native Android folders from your `app.json`:

```bash
npx expo prebuild --platform android
```

This creates an `android/` folder with a regular Android project. If you also want iOS, run `npx expo prebuild` without `--platform`.

> Before you do, check that `android.package` in `app.json` has your app's identifier (for example `com.yourcompany.yourapp`). More details in the [prebuild docs](https://docs.expo.dev/workflow/prebuild/).

## Step 2: build the APK

### Option A: with Gradle from the terminal (the fastest)

Go into the `android/` folder and run Gradle:

```bash
cd android
./gradlew assembleRelease
```

On Windows, use `gradlew.bat assembleRelease`.

When it finishes, your APK is at:

```
android/app/build/outputs/apk/release/app-release.apk
```

You can install that file on any Android device. By default, the project Expo generates signs the release build with a test (debug) key, so it's fine for testing and sharing, but **not for publishing on Google Play** (see below).

If you only want a development build, use `./gradlew assembleDebug`. The APK ends up in `apk/debug/app-debug.apk`, but it needs the Metro server running to load the JavaScript.

### Option B: with Android Studio

1. Open the `android/` folder in Android Studio (**File → Open**).
2. Wait for the Gradle sync to finish.
3. Go to **Build → Generate App Bundles or APKs → Generate APKs**.
4. When it's done, click **locate** in the notification to open the APK's folder.

To switch between debug and release, use **Build → Select Build Variant**.

### Option C: EAS Build on your own computer

If you already use EAS, you can run the same build on your machine, with no queue and without using up your plan's builds:

```bash
npm install -g eas-cli
eas build --platform android --profile preview --local
```

To get an APK instead of an AAB, the profile in your `eas.json` needs:

```json
{
  "build": {
    "preview": {
      "android": { "buildType": "apk" }
    }
  }
}
```

## To publish on Google Play

Google Play doesn't accept APKs signed with the test key, and it asks for the **AAB** format. You need to:

1. Create your own key (keystore):

```bash
keytool -genkeypair -v -storetype PKCS12 -keystore my-app.keystore -alias my-app -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000
```

2. Set it up in `android/app/build.gradle`, under `signingConfigs`, and use it for the `release` build. The exact steps are in the [React Native guide](https://reactnative.dev/docs/signed-apk-android).
3. Build the bundle:

```bash
./gradlew bundleRelease
```

> Keep the keystore and its passwords somewhere safe. If you lose it, you can't publish updates to your app with the same key.

## Common errors

- **"SDK location not found"**: create `android/local.properties` with the line `sdk.dir=/path/to/your/Android/sdk`.
- **Java version error**: Gradle needs Java 17. Check with `java -version`.
- **I changed `app.json` and nothing changed**: run `npx expo prebuild --platform android --clean` again. Careful: `--clean` deletes any changes you made by hand in `android/`.

## Summary

| I want... | Command |
| --- | --- |
| An APK to test and share | `./gradlew assembleRelease` |
| To do it with a GUI | Android Studio → Generate APKs |
| To use my EAS config without paying | `eas build --local` |
| To publish on Google Play | Own key + `./gradlew bundleRelease` |
